---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Env from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Env.astro";
import Sidenote from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Sidenote.astro";
import M from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/M.astro";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Stability from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Stability.astro";
import Figure from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Figure.astro";
import PostHeader from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/PostHeader.astro";
import CellSketch from "../../components/CellSketch.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";
---
<Docs title="Long-form writing" status="experimental" lede="Notes, explainers, and blog posts: the post header, sidenotes, margin figures, footnotes, theorem environments, and collapsible detail.">
  <h2>Post header</h2>
  <PostHeader level={3} title="Example note: the Hodge Laplacian, drawn" lede="A worked example on one triangle." date="2026-09-14" minutes={6} tags={["Topology"]} />
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<PostHeader
  title="Example note: the Hodge Laplacian, drawn"
  lede="A worked example on one triangle."
  date="2026-09-14"
  minutes={6}
  tags={["Topology"]}
/>`} />
  <p>The title is set like a paper's, so a site's posts and papers read as one series. The date prints in full, as "14 September 2026"; pass <code>locale</code> for another language. In an <code>&lt;Article&gt;</code>, give it <code>slot="header"</code> and it lines up with the text. The <a href={u("/patterns/post/")}>blog post pattern</a> shows the whole page.</p>

  <h2>Theorem environments</h2>
  <Env kind="definition" num="2.1" name="Simplicial complex">
    A simplicial complex <M tex={String.raw`K`} /> on a vertex set <M tex={String.raw`V`} /> is a set of nonempty finite subsets of <M tex={String.raw`V`} /> such that every nonempty subset of a member is also a member.
  </Env>
  <Env kind="theorem" num="2.2">
    The kernel of the Hodge Laplacian <M tex={String.raw`L_k`} /> is isomorphic to the <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-th homology group of <M tex={String.raw`K`} /> over the reals.
  </Env>
  <Env kind="proof">
    The kernel of <M tex={String.raw`L_k`} /> is the intersection of the kernels of <M tex={String.raw`B_k`} /> and <M tex={String.raw`B_{k+1}^{\top}`} />, which are the harmonic <M tex={String.raw`k`} />-chains, and these represent homology classes one to one.
  </Env>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Env kind="definition" num="2.1" name="Simplicial complex">...</Env>
<Env kind="theorem" num="2.2">...</Env>
<Env kind="proof">...</Env>`} />
  <p>Kinds: definition, theorem, lemma, proposition, corollary, remark, example, proof. Theorems and lemmas set their body in italics, as print does; definitions sit on the wall gray; proofs end with a tomato box.</p>

  <h2>Sidenotes</h2>
  <p>A sidenote keeps a remark next to the sentence it belongs to.<Sidenote n={1}>When the page has room after the text, the note sits there, in the margin. On a narrow screen it drops below the line.</Sidenote> Use them for asides a curious reader wants and a hurried one can skip.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<p>A sentence.<Sidenote n={1}>The aside.</Sidenote> More text.</p>`} />

  <h2>In the margin</h2>
  <Figure margin num={1}>
    <CellSketch />
    <Fragment slot="caption">A 2-cell and its three oriented edges.</Fragment>
  </Figure>
  <p>A small figure, table, or code block can sit in the same margin as the sidenotes, beside the paragraph after it. Place it before that paragraph and give it <code>.ply-margin</code>, or pass <code>margin</code> to <code>&lt;Figure&gt;</code>. Where the margin is closed, it stays in the text, above that paragraph.</p>
  <Code lang="astro" code={`<Figure margin num={1}>
  <img src={sketch.src} alt="A filled triangle with oriented edges." />
  <Fragment slot="caption">A 2-cell and its three oriented edges.</Fragment>
</Figure>
<p>The paragraph it sits beside.</p>`} />
  <p>Keep margin blocks small: 14rem wide is room for a sketch, a short table, or a few lines of code. Anything larger belongs in the <a href={u("/foundations/layout/#articles")}>text</a>.</p>

  <h2>Collapsible detail</h2>
  <details class="ply-details">
    <summary>Derivation of the update rule</summary>
    <p>Long derivations, extra tables, and setup details go here, closed by default, so the main argument reads straight through.</p>
  </details>
  <Code lang="html" code={`<details class="ply-details">
  <summary>Derivation of the update rule</summary>
  <p>...</p>
</details>`} />

  <h2>Quotes</h2>
  <blockquote class="ply-quote">
    Quotes keep a thin rule and a larger size, and the source goes underneath.
    <cite>Source, year</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Footnotes</h2>
  <p>For references that should not interrupt the line, use a numbered list in <code>.ply-footnotes</code> at the end of the article.</p>
</Docs>
